{#
   THE CATALOGUE SHELL — S59's shape, once, for every list of things.

   Machines, espaces, objets prêtables, matériaux, annuaires: the *layout* of a
   catalogue is identical and only the vocabulary differs. This file owns the
   layout. Callers supply words and data; **they never supply markup**, and this
   file never asks what kind of thing it is listing.

   ⚠️ THE RULE THAT KEEPS THIS USEFUL: the moment this template contains
   `{% if entity == 'machine' %}` it has stopped being a shell and become a
   fifth page wearing four disguises. Anything that varies per entity is a
   parameter. If something cannot be expressed as a parameter, add a *block* —
   not a branch.

   Use it with `{% embed %}`, filling `cards` (and `filters` if the page wants
   more than a search box):

     {% embed 'site/_catalogue.html.twig' with {
         route: 'app_places', title: 'Espaces'|trans, tiles: tiles,
         search: search, filter_key: 'cat', filter_value: category,
         total: total, all: all, headline_count: freeCount,
         headline_label: 'places.available_now'|trans,
         search_placeholder: 'places.search'|trans,
     } %}
         {% block cards %}…{% endblock %}
     {% endembed %}

   Parameters, all optional except `route` and `title`:

     route             the page's own route name — used for every self-link, so
                       the shell never hardcodes a URL
     title             page heading
     tiles             [{slug, label, total, free}] — the category filter bar.
                       Omit entirely and the bar is not rendered.
     tile_all_label    the "all" tile's word ("Toutes", "Tous")
     filter_key        query parameter the tiles write ('cat')
     filter_value      the currently selected one
     filter_all_value  what the "all" tile writes. Default '' — a bare URL, which
                       is right when no filter means everything. ⚠️ Pass a value
                       when the page's DEFAULT is itself a filter: /events opens
                       on upcoming, so its bare URL is not "all" and an all-tile
                       linking there would light up while showing a subset.
     search            current search string
     search_placeholder
     total / all       counts for the sentence
     unit / unit_plural  the noun being counted ("machine"/"machines")
     headline_count    the one big number, omitted when zero
     headline_label    the words after it
     empty_text        the empty state's sentence
     card_min          minimum card width, e.g. '29rem'. Omit for the default
                       16rem (four across). Bigger means fewer per row — this is
                       the ONE knob for grid density and it writes a custom
                       property, so no page ever declares its own grid rule.

   ⚠️ Counts in `tiles` are expected to be computed over the UNFILTERED set, so
   that choosing one category does not blank out every other tile's number.
#}
{% set route = route %}
{% set filter_key = filter_key|default('cat') %}
{% set filter_value = filter_value|default('') %}
{% set search = search|default('') %}
{% set tiles = tiles|default([]) %}
{# 🔴 **Every control has to carry every OTHER control's value.** `base_query`
   used to be the search term and nothing else, so choosing a location and then
   clicking a category tile threw the location away — the operator hit exactly
   that: bâtiment D, then Fraisage, and bâtiment D was gone.

   It is built once here and used by the tile links, the "all" tile and the search
   form, so there is one place to add a filter to rather than three to remember.
   A tile still overrides its OWN key on top of this. #}
{% set base_query = search ? { (search_key|default('q')): search } : {} %}
{% if venue_context|default(null) and venue_context.venues|length > 1 and venue_context.location != 'all' %}
    {% set base_query = base_query|merge({location: venue_context.location}) %}
{% endif %}
{# ⚠️ `|filter`, not an inline `if` on the for tag — removed in Twig 3, and it
   fails at LINT, taking the whole template down rather than one row. #}
{% for field in refine|default([])|filter(f => f.value|default('') is not empty) %}
    {% set base_query = base_query|merge({ (field.name): field.value }) %}
{% endfor %}

{# ⚠️ **The frame is the catalogue's only shape (S138c, operator 2026-08-16).**
   `frame: 'full'` (S137, variant E) puts the title, count, search and tiles
   inside one panel, the shape the admin's list page uses. It was opt-in while
   `/machines` was the trial ground; **all nine callers passed it**, so the
   unframed branch was already unreachable code describing a page nobody could
   see. The parameter is gone rather than kept as a second catalogue shape.

   ⚠️ Measured on the proposal at an 886 px column: **149 px before the first
   card, against 168 px unframed.** Framing is not the same as spending height —
   putting the title, the count and the search on one line buys back more than
   the padding costs.

   ⚠️ `{% block filters %}` went with the unframed branch: it only ever rendered
   there, and no caller defined it. `{% block head_extra %}` and
   `{% block before_grid %}` stay — the first is used by `/mes-reservations`, the
   second is an extension point that renders wherever it is defined. #}
<main class="ml-page is-framed">
<div class="ml-wrap">

    <div class="ml-panel">

    <div class="ml-head">
        {# ⚠️ A subtitle was tried here and dropped (operator, 2026-08-12). It is
           removed rather than left switched off: a parameter nobody passes is a
           thing the next reader has to evaluate before they can ignore it. The
           title carries the page on its own. #}
        <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
        {# The count and the search join the title on its line — that is where the
           height comes back from.

           ⚠️ **One number pair, not two.** It used to read "10 available now"
           beside "11 machines": two counts, two nouns, and the reader has to work
           out that one is a subset of the other. "10/11 disponibles" says the
           same thing in one glance, and the green stays on the 10 — the figure a
           member actually came for is the one that is free, and it is the only
           part that earns a colour. #}
        <span class="ml-head-count">
            {%- if headline_count is defined and headline_label|default('') -%}
                {# The slash is its own element so it can be given even optical
                   side-bearings. Butted straight against two numerals it sits
                   tight on one side and loose on the other, which is what made
                   the pair look mis-set. #}
                <strong class="ml-head-free">{{ headline_count }}</strong><span class="ml-head-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>{{ all|default(total|default(0)) }} {{ available_word|default('common.available_short'|trans) }}
            {%- else -%}
                {{ total|default(0) }} {{ (total|default(0)) > 1 ? (unit_plural|default('common.result_many'|trans)) : (unit|default('common.result_one'|trans)) }}
            {%- endif -%}
        </span>
        <form class="ml-head-search" method="get" action="{{ path(route) }}" role="search">
            {# Same rule as the tiles: searching must not silently drop the
               location or a refine menu. `base_query` minus the search key,
               which this form owns. #}
            {% if filter_value %}<input type="hidden" name="{{ filter_key }}" value="{{ filter_value }}">{% endif %}
            {% for key, value in base_query|filter((value, key) => key != search_key|default('q')) %}
                <input type="hidden" name="{{ key }}" value="{{ value }}">
            {% endfor %}
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="7"/><path d="m21 21-4.3-4.3"/></svg>
            <label class="sr-only" for="ml-q">{{ 'common.search'|trans }}</label>
            <input id="ml-q" type="search" name="{{ search_key|default('q') }}" value="{{ search }}" placeholder="{{ search_placeholder|default('') }}">
            <button class="sr-only" type="submit">{{ 'common.search'|trans }}</button>
            {% if search or filter_value %}<a class="ml-head-clear" href="{{ path(route) }}" title="{{ 'common.reset'|trans }}"><span aria-hidden="true">×</span></a>{% endif %}
        </form>
        {% block head_extra %}{% endblock %}
    </div>

    {# The filter bar. Persistent tiles rather than section headings: grouping a
       grid by category leaves a group of one as a row of one, which on a small
       catalogue is three empty cells per category, all the way down. #}
    {% if tiles is not empty %}
        <nav class="ml-cats" aria-label="{{ tiles_label|default(title) }}">
            {% set all_value = filter_all_value|default('') %}
            <a class="ml-tile{{ filter_value == all_value ? ' is-on' : '' }}"
               href="{{ path(route, all_value ? base_query|merge({ (filter_key): all_value }) : base_query) }}">
                <span class="ml-tile-label">{{ tile_all_label|default('common.all_f'|trans) }}</span>
                <span class="ml-tile-count">{{ all|default(total|default(0)) }}</span>
            </a>
            {% for tile in tiles %}
                <a class="ml-tile{{ filter_value == tile.slug ? ' is-on' : '' }}"
                   href="{{ path(route, base_query|merge({ (filter_key): tile.slug })) }}">
                    {# ⚠️ NOT `show_tile_icons|default(true)`. Twig's `default`
                       fires on empty as well as undefined, so `false` came back
                       as `true` and the one caller that switched icons off got
                       them anyway. Any boolean parameter here needs this shape. #}
                    {% if tile.slug is defined and (show_tile_icons is defined ? show_tile_icons : true) %}
                        <span class="ml-tile-icon">
                            {% include 'site/_category_icon.html.twig' with { slug: tile.slug } only %}
                        </span>
                    {% endif %}
                    <span class="ml-tile-label">{{ tile.label }}</span>
                    <span class="ml-tile-count">
                        {{ tile.total }}{% if tile.free|default(0) > 0 %} · <span class="ml-free">{{ tile.free }} {{ free_word|default('libres') }}</span>{% endif %}
                    </span>
                </a>
            {% endfor %}
        </nav>
    {% endif %}

    {# The "Affiner" tier, the same shape the admin filter panel uses: labelled
       dropdowns that apply on change, **location first**, and the whole group
       absent when there is nothing to refine by. A public catalogue and an admin
       list ask the same question of a member and an operator — "narrow this" —
       and there is no reason for them to answer it with two different controls.

       ⚠️ The location group disappears on a single-location install, decided
       inside this block so no caller can forget it. Same rule as `_admin_filters`. #}
    {% set _venues = venue_context|default(null) and venue_context.venues|length > 1 %}
    {% set _refine = refine|default([]) %}
    {% if _venues or _refine is not empty %}
        <form class="ml-refine" method="get" action="{{ path(route) }}">
            <span class="ml-refine-label">{{ 'admin_list.refine'|trans }}</span>
            {% if filter_value %}<input type="hidden" name="{{ filter_key }}" value="{{ filter_value }}">{% endif %}
            {% if search %}<input type="hidden" name="{{ search_key|default('q') }}" value="{{ search }}">{% endif %}
            {% if _venues %}
                <label class="ml-select{{ venue_context.location != 'all' ? ' is-set' : '' }}">
                    <span class="ml-select-k">{{ 'venue_context.menu'|trans }}</span>
                    <select name="location" data-controller="autosubmit" data-action="change->autosubmit#submit">
                        <option value="all">{{ 'venue_context.menu_all'|trans }}</option>
                        {% for venue in venue_context.venues %}
                            <option value="{{ venue.slug }}"{{ venue_context.location == venue.slug ? ' selected' : '' }}>{{ venue.name }}</option>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </select>
                </label>
            {% endif %}
            {% for field in _refine %}
                <label class="ml-select{{ field.value|default('') is not empty ? ' is-set' : '' }}">
                    <span class="ml-select-k">{{ field.label }}</span>
                    <select name="{{ field.name }}" data-controller="autosubmit" data-action="change->autosubmit#submit">
                        {% for option in field.options %}
                            <option value="{{ option.value }}"{{ field.value|default('') == option.value ? ' selected' : '' }}>{{ option.label }}</option>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </select>
                </label>
            {% endfor %}
            <noscript><button class="ml-btn" type="submit">{{ 'common.confirm'|trans }}</button></noscript>
        </form>
    {% endif %}

    {# ⚠️ **The frame stops here — the cards stay outside it.** The first pass
       wrapped the grid too (variant E on the proposal page). A frame around
       cards that are themselves frames is a border inside a border, and on a
       long catalogue the outer one runs off the screen and stops closing
       anything visible. Framed now means: what you DECIDE with is in a box,
       what you LOOK at is not — the same frontier the admin draws between its
       filter panel and its rows. #}
    </div>

    {% block before_grid %}{% endblock %}

    {% if total|default(0) == 0 %}
        {# An empty state offers the way out; it never just says "rien". #}
        <p class="ml-empty">
            {{ empty_text|default('common.no_result'|trans) }}
            <a href="{{ path(route) }}">{{ 'common.reset'|trans }}</a>
        </p>
    {% endif %}

    {# `reveal_after: N` shows the first N cards and hides the rest behind one
       button. A shell feature rather than a per-page one: every catalogue here can
       grow long, and the seven of them reinventing a toggle is how the "voir plus"
       on one page ends up behaving differently from the next.

       ⚠️ The extra cards stay in the DOM, hidden by CSS — so in-page search, print
       and screen readers still reach them, and no-JS degrades to "everything is
       shown" rather than "half the catalogue is missing". #}
    {# ⚠️ Grid and button share one wrapper because a Stimulus target must live
       inside its controller's element — with the controller on `.ml-grid` the
       button would simply never be found, and silently. #}
    <div{% if reveal_after|default(0) > 0 %} data-controller="reveal" data-reveal-limit-value="{{ reveal_after }}"{% endif %}>
        <div class="ml-grid"{% if card_min|default('') %} style="--ml-card-min: {{ card_min }};"{% endif %}>
            {% block cards %}{% endblock %}
        </div>

        {% if reveal_after|default(0) > 0 and (total|default(0)) > reveal_after %}
            <div class="ml-more">
                <button type="button" class="ml-btn ml-btn-quiet"
                        data-action="reveal#toggle"
                        data-reveal-target="button"
                        data-reveal-more-value="{{ reveal_more_label|default('Voir plus') }}"
                        data-reveal-less-value="{{ reveal_less_label|default('Voir moins') }}">{{ reveal_more_label|default('Voir plus') }}</button>
            </div>
        {% endif %}
    </div>

    {% block after_grid %}{% endblock %}

</div>
</main>
